From 8c75724431e259466234e532da5fc1b84ac40ef9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 09:34:39 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] bt-a: give A2DP an audio module that exists, and start
 Bluetooth off

trout's audio policy includes a2dp_audio_policy_configuration.xml, whose
module is named "a2dp". There is no audio.a2dp.default anywhere in AOSP
17 -- the implementation is audio.bluetooth.default and its module name
is "bluetooth". Left alone, a phone pairs, connects, and media routes
"successfully" while nothing comes out of the speaker.

device/google/trout/aosp_trout_common.mk wraps its copy list in
`ifndef LOCAL_AUDIO_PRODUCT_COPY_FILES`, so defining that variable here
replaces the list wholesale. It has to be assigned ABOVE the inherit,
for the reason the comment at the top of the product makefile gives: a
parent's guard can be pre-empted from there. That comment gains one
paragraph: LOCAL_* audio variables are plain global Make variables, not
product variables, so clear-var-list in node_fns.mk never resets them
and the value set here survives into trout's file.

Four differences from trout's five entries:

  * audio_policy_configuration.xml is forked into this device, to
    include the bluetooth module instead of the a2dp one.
  * a2dp_audio_policy_configuration.xml is dropped and
    bluetooth_audio_policy_configuration.xml copied instead -- a local
    copy of upstream's file, so the SCO/HFP ports can be added in one
    place. The non-_7_0 variant is required: the top-level document is
    version="1.0" and lists samplingRates comma-separated, while the
    _7_0 files use spaces.
  * The three XIncludes trout references but never ships
    (r_submix_audio_policy_configuration.xml, audio_policy_volumes.xml,
    default_volume_tables.xml) are now shipped. Serializer.cpp only
    ALOGEs a failed XInclude and carries on, and EngineBase then fell
    back to gDefaultVolumeGroups ("No legacy volume group found, using
    default music group"), because parseLegacyVolumes() reads the
    <volumes> section of this very file. So the guest ran with ONE
    volume group and no r_submix module while audio.r_submix.default.so
    was installed all along.
    THIS CHANGES AUDIO BEHAVIOUR ON PURPOSE: the guest gains the real
    legacy volume curves. The check on the board is that the media
    slider still behaves on the HDMI bus outputs and "No legacy volume
    group found" no longer appears -- `dumpsys audio` before and after.
  * car_audio_configuration.xml, android.hardware.broadcastradio.xml and
    usb_audio_policy_configuration.xml are unchanged from trout,
    restated because defining the variable replaces the whole list.

The image also lacked the module the policy names. PRODUCT_PACKAGES
gains audio.bluetooth.default, the HW module AudioFlinger loads, and
android.hardware.bluetooth.audio-impl, the AIDL provider factory the
Bluetooth stack talks to, which the HIDL audio HAL service this image
already ships dlopens.

The HCI transport is not here: it is a virtio-serial port owned by
DomD, and the DomD side of it is a separate change.

Bluetooth also starts off on a fresh /data. SettingsProvider seeds
bluetooth_on from def_bluetooth_on on the first boot of an empty /data.
The device overlay has said false since this device was added
(2026-08-23), but CarSettingsProviderConfigRRO sets it true and a
runtime overlay outranks a build-time one: on a fresh RPi4 /data
(2026-09-24) bluetooth_on came up 1 and `cmd overlay lookup` resolved
def_bluetooth_on to true.

Add DomaSettingsProviderRRO, a product RRO at priority 1 that holds
only def_bluetooth_on=false. Static overlays are applied in partition
order and then by priority, and Car's RRO is in product with none, so
this one is applied after it; a vendor RRO would lose whatever its
priority. The device overlay's own def_bluetooth_on, which never took
effect, goes, and so do the notes around it, including the one about a
guest with no Bluetooth transport, which DomD's HCI proxy has since
provided. That overlay now overrides two values, the animation scales.

Nothing forces Bluetooth back on: the boot path and BluetoothPowerPolicy
restore the saved state only.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending [automotive-grade-linux/Android_device_sodev_xenvm-cf]
